WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.240
(orchestral music)

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.620
- [Ross] When you're
watching a television show

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.720
or a movie, and you see an actor

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.480
holding a magazine, or
a book, or a letter,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.340
a lot of what you're seeing on the screen

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.800
was custom-made by people like me.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.250
- Let me see their paperwork.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.720
- If I've done my job right

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.400
in creating that piece of paper,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.960
the possibilities are literally endless.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.710
I mean, one single piece
of paper can communicate

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.800
a huge amount in telling the audience

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.453
about the character or about the scene.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.370
It's figuring out how this thing

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.173
can best do its job of
being a part of the story.

00:00:51.669 --> 00:00:54.580
(lively orchestral music)

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.450
My name is Ross MacDonald,
and I create paper props

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.783
for movies and television.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.830
When I was a kid, I was
obsessed with comics,

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.450
was obsessed with paper and illustration,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.070
and really loved that
world that I was reading

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.723
or seeing on paper.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.190
I've done a little bit of everything.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.580
For years, I worked as a magazine

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.760
and newspaper illustrator,
book illustrator.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.343
That eventually led me into making props.

00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:28.220
(soft marimba music)

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.400
For the last 25 years,
I've made tens of thousands

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.713
of paper props for close to 50
television shows and movies.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.400
My first major motion
picture was "Baby's Day Out,"

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.540
and I was hired, basically,
as an illustrator

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.310
to illustrate a faux
1930's children's book.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.350
- [Man] Take the book.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.100
- I've done literally tens of thousands

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.900
of props for all five seasons
of "Boardwalk Empire."

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.000
I did the Pawnee Charter for an episode

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.430
of "Parks and Recreation."

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.610
For "Joy," I did all of the patents

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.000
for the mops that we see.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.629
For "Hateful Eight," I did
the Red Apple Tobacco tin.

00:02:01.629 --> 00:02:03.879
(chatters)

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.030
Book.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.060
I've designed everything from passports,

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.340
and maps, and travel
documents, wanted posters,

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.560
government forms, FBI reports,

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.030
psychiatric files, agent
identification folders,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.543
anything that's made of paper,

00:02:33.543 --> 00:02:35.043
I made it one time or another.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.080
Part of what you need to figure out

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:43.240
when you're making a paper prop

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.396
is the backstory of the piece of paper.

00:02:45.396 --> 00:02:46.229
(lively orchestral music)

00:02:46.229 --> 00:02:48.240
You don't just take a new book,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.530
and slosh brown ink on
it to make it look old.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.950
You gotta figure out how it got worn,

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.590
how it got dirty.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:56.850
The scene might involve an actor

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.480
reading three lines out of a letter,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.500
but you have to figure out, how old is it?

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.140
What kind of paper is it made out of?

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.610
How was the paper made?

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.090
Should it be made with carbon paper?

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.490
Should it be on onion skin?

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.730
Is some guy gonna pull out

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.470
a rumpled piece of paper from his pocket,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.550
or is he gonna pull it
out of a file folder?

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:13.990
What color paper should it be?

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.460
Should it be yellow, should it be white,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.050
should it be ivory-colored?

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.030
Who's signing it?

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.740
What does their handwriting look like?

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.670
What kind of ink are they
using when they write it?

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.730
What kind of pen are they using?

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.710
And then, because no
letter is ever three lines,

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.970
you have to write the rest
of the copy in the letter.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.070
Depending on what I'm making,

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:36.070
a project can take, you know,
a day, an hour, weeks, months.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.670
Every page is filled out.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.290
Every book has text on it, and pictures.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.060
Every paper in the file folder

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.223
is a real document that's
all filled out and created.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.670
For example, for "The Book Of Secrets,"

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.160
they said, "Just come up with secrets,"

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.027
so I said, "OK, well, let's do Roswell,

00:03:53.027 --> 00:03:55.397
"let's do the Kennedy assassination.

00:03:55.397 --> 00:03:58.290
"Let's do all these crazy
conspiracy theory things."

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.220
Then, there was the research
into those specific things.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.060
So, you have to find all that,

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.603
figure out how to
recreate it convincingly.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:06.810
It was a huge undertaking.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.730
It took about four months
to really build that prop,

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:13.963
and it's just communicated in
a split second in the scene.

00:04:16.772 --> 00:04:19.210
- Let's see here, what do we got here?

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:20.860
- Have you seen the evening paper?

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.131
- I have here pieces of paper.

00:04:22.131 --> 00:04:24.290
(soft orchestral music)

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.120
- There's so many amazing things

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.663
that start off with a piece of paper,

00:04:29.663 --> 00:04:32.763
and the props that I'm
making are all part of that.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:36.140
Sometimes, when I go to the theater,

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.893
I'm so excited, I'm almost shaking,

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.720
and it's not because I want to see

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.680
the driver's license I
made for one of the actors.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.380
It's because I did a tiny
bit of work on that movie,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.323
and that's very exciting to me.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.840
In the end, you've
produced this thing, which,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.130
you know, you've done
your best job of nailing,

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.070
getting absolutely right,
and, you know, sometimes,

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:04.547
I'm just like, "Wow."

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.970
When you see something like
that in the final product,

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.020
and realize that this thing you worked on

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:16.600
is this huge moment in the story,

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.893
that can be just really,
really a great feeling.

00:05:25.844 --> 00:05:28.761
(electronic music)

